      <p>For readers comparing ecosystems, these regional splits are part of why Kobo keeps gaining ground on Kindle outside the U.S. — we covered the retail shift in <a href="/blog/kobo-sales-double-us-retail-2026/">Kobo's U.S. sales doubling</a> and the platform mechanics in <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook</a>.</p>

      <h2>Romance Is Branching Out</h2>
      <p>Contemporary-romance authors are crossing genre lines. Ali Hazelwood, Laurie Gilmore, and B.K. Borison are moving into magical realism with commercial success, suggesting romance readers want "a touch of the ethereal." On the indie side, Kobo Writing Life data shows independent authors nurturing niche genres — paranormal romance sub-tropes in particular — before they hit the mainstream. The trad-wife fascination that powered <em>Yesteryear</em> is expected to deepen.</p>

      <h2>Kobo's 2027 Reading Forecast</h2>
      <p>Kobo's literary experts flagged four emerging shifts for next year:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Folk horror boom</strong> — ancient anxieties and rural myths tapping into the current mood.</li>
        <li><strong>Dystopian returns</strong> — a major resurgence of dystopian and post-apocalyptic fiction as the political and ecopolitical climate shifts.</li>
        <li><strong>Trad-life fascination</strong> — domestic subversion as a fiction genre, growing out of <em>Yesteryear</em>'s success.</li>
        <li><strong>Genre-blending romantasy</strong> — hybrid genres mixing romantasy with political intrigue, myth retellings, and mystery elements, while the genre's mainstays (Rebecca Yarros, Sarah J. Maas) keep dominating.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What This Means for EPUB Readers</h2>
      <p>A fragmented, community-driven market is good news for readers who buy DRM-free. Niche titles thrive on stores that sell open formats — Kobo, Kobo Writing Life, and DRM-free indie shops — because the readers driving these trends skew technical and mobile. If you're building a personal library around this year's trends, the practical playbook hasn't changed: buy the EPUB, sideload or sync it to your reader of choice, and keep a conversion tool handy for the stragglers. Our <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">Send to Kindle walkthrough</a> covers the sideloading path, the <a href="/blog/kobo-stack-send-to-kobo-paid-sync-2026/">Kobo Stack deep-dive</a> explains Kobo's new paid sync tier, and the <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free library guide</a> maps the stores where these niche titles are easiest to own outright. For titles locked in other ecosystems, <a href="/blog/kindle-to-epub/">Kindle-to-EPUB conversion</a> remains the standard escape hatch.</p>

      <p>The full report is published on Kobo's blog; Good e-Reader's coverage of the announcement is the fastest English-language summary if you want the raw trend list.</p>

